To implement the `findZero` method, you can use numerical methods such as the Newton-Raphson method to find a root of the polynomial. Here's how you can do it:

```java
    public double findZero(List<Double> xs) {
        // Initial guess can be 0 or any other value
        double x = 0;
        double tolerance = 1e-7; // Define a tolerance level for convergence
        int maxIterations = 1000; // Maximum number of iterations to prevent infinite loops

        for (int i = 0; i < maxIterations; i++) {
            double fx = poly(xs, x); // Calculate f(x)
            double dfx = derivative(xs, x); // Calculate f'(x)

            if (Math.abs(dfx) < tolerance) {
                throw new ArithmeticException("Derivative near zero, no convergence possible.");
            }

            double nextX = x - fx / dfx; // Newton-Raphson formula

            if (Math.abs(nextX - x) < tolerance) {
                return nextX; // Convergence achieved
            }

            x = nextX;
        }

        throw new ArithmeticException("Maximum iterations reached, no convergence.");
    }

    /**
     * Calculates the derivative of the polynomial at point x.
     */
    private double derivative(List<Double> xs, double x) {
        double result = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < xs.size(); i++) {
            result += i * xs.get(i) * Math.pow(x, i - 1);
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

